<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Schleswig</h1><p class="st-meta">1058 CE – 1866 CE (approx.) · capital Schleswig</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Duchy of southern Jutland on the Danish-German border, held by Danish royal princes and Holstein counts and fought over in the 19th-century Schleswig wars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Southern Jutland</a> was the frontier of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark</a>, guarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a> earthworks and home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>. From the 11th century it was governed as a jarldom or duchy by members of the royal family; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canute-lavard">Canute Lavard</a> held it from 1115, and the dukes descended from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-king-of-denmark">Abel</a> made it a hereditary fief.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy straddled Danish and German worlds: Danish speech dominated in the north, German and Frisian in the south, and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlei">Schlei</a> was its capital. From 1386 the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-holstein">Holstein</a> held it, and in 1460 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ribe">Treaty of Ribe</a> made Christian I its duke, promising Schleswig and Holstein would stay forever undivided. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Gottorp</a> Castle became a seat of a ducal line.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Rising Danish and German nationalism led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-schleswig-war">First Schleswig War</a> in 1848-1851 and the Second in 1864, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> ceded the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Prussia annexed it in 1866-1867. A 1920 plebiscite returned northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> to Denmark, while the south remains part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-schleswig.jpg" alt="Duchy of Schleswig" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Tinkaer1991 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Duchy_of_Schleswig-Holstein_18th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1058 CE – 1866 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schleswig</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Canute Lavard, Abel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic, then Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Danish, Low German, North Frisian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Jutland, Sønderjylland, Herzogtum Schleswig</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1462&amp;focus=duchy-of-schleswig" aria-label="Duchy of Schleswig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4891 311 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Schleswig lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5211 567L5274 535M5211 567L5274 535M5211 567L5209 586M5211 567L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5274 535h0M5274 535h0M5209 586h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 567h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 567h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schleswig. <a href="/atlas?year=1462&amp;focus=duchy-of-schleswig">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
